{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
<div class="docs-page-with-sidebar">
  <nav class="docs-side-nav" aria-label="On this page">
    <h2>Foundations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#contract">Foundation contract</a></li>
      <li><a href="#colour">Semantic colour</a></li>
      <li><a href="#typography">Typography roles</a></li>
      <li><a href="#layout">Layout &amp; spacing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#interaction">Interaction</a></li>
      <li><a href="#themes">Theme implementations</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
  <div>
    <span class="docs-eyebrow">Core system</span>
    <h1>Foundations define roles, not brands</h1>
    <p class="docs-lede">The core system specifies the decisions every implementation must make. It does not prescribe an organisation's colours, fonts or CSS namespace. Those values belong to a named implementation theme.</p>

    <div class="docs-module" id="contract">
      <h2>Foundation contract</h2>
      <div class="docs-grid docs-grid--3">
        <div class="docs-card" style="cursor:default;"><span class="docs-eyebrow">Stable</span><h3>Semantic roles</h3><p>Define what a token or treatment means and where it may be used.</p></div>
        <div class="docs-card" style="cursor:default;"><span class="docs-eyebrow">Variable</span><h3>Theme values</h3><p>Assign brand-specific colour, type, radius, spacing and motion values.</p></div>
        <div class="docs-card" style="cursor:default;"><span class="docs-eyebrow">Required</span><h3>Accessible behaviour</h3><p>Preserve contrast, focus, keyboard, reading-order and reduced-motion requirements.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="docs-note"><strong>Core rule:</strong> components consume semantic roles. They must not assume that one organisation's palette, typography or class prefix is universal.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-module" id="colour">
      <h2>Semantic colour</h2>
      <table class="docs-field-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Constraint</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Primary action</td><td>Links, primary controls and selected states.</td><td>Must meet contrast requirements in every state.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Accent</td><td>Small emphasis, markers and focus support.</td><td>Never communicates meaning alone or dominates large surfaces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Surface</td><td>Page, card, panel and elevated backgrounds.</td><td>Maintains hierarchy without unnecessary decoration.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Text</td><td>Heading, body, muted and inverse content.</td><td>Supports sustained reading and clear information hierarchy.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Status</td><td>Success, warning, error and informational feedback.</td><td>Always paired with explicit text or another non-colour cue.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-module" id="typography">
      <h2>Typography roles</h2>
      <table class="docs-field-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Use</th><th>Required behaviour</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Display</td><td>Course or major section openers.</td><td>Reserved for short, high-priority statements.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heading</td><td>Page, section, component and activity hierarchy.</td><td>Logical levels remain clear independently of visual size.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Body</td><td>Explanations, instructions and feedback.</td><td>Readable measure, line height and responsive sizing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Label</td><td>Eyebrows, metadata, status and control labels.</td><td>Concise wording with sufficient size and contrast.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-module" id="layout">
      <h2>Layout and spacing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use a small, consistent spacing scale rather than arbitrary one-off values.</li>
        <li>Define narrow, standard, wide and full content measures by learning purpose.</li>
        <li>Allow components to reflow without changing their meaning or reading order.</li>
        <li>Use radius and elevation consistently and only where they clarify grouping or hierarchy.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-module" id="interaction">
      <h2>Interaction foundations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>All operable elements expose a visible keyboard focus state.</li>
        <li>Controls use familiar labels and retain their meaning across implementations.</li>
        <li>Feedback appears close to the action that produced it and remains available to assistive technology.</li>
        <li>Motion supports orientation, state change or continuity and respects reduced-motion preferences.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-module" id="themes">
      <h2>Theme implementations</h2>
      <p>Each organisation or product maps these roles to its own governed values and technical namespace. Theme documentation records those values without redefining the shared learning purpose.</p>
      <div class="docs-grid docs-grid--2">
        <a class="docs-card" href="{{ base }}implementations/index.html">
          <div class="docs-card__top"><h3>Implementation directory</h3><span class="docs-tag">Available</span></div>
          <p>Browse governed organisational themes, platform expressions and production assets.</p>
        </a>
        <div class="docs-card" style="cursor:default;">
          <div class="docs-card__top"><h3>Future themes</h3><span class="docs-tag">Available</span></div>
          <p>Additional organisational or product themes can be added without altering the core system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
